    <section class="lede reveal">
      <p class="drop">Most parents already do the careful things. They sterilise the bottle. They warm the feed. They choose &ldquo;BPA-free.&rdquo; They read the label twice. The uncomfortable part is that the most common bottle material &mdash; polypropylene plastic &mdash; was the same material studied when researchers measured high microplastic release during normal formula-preparation steps.</p>
      <p>The point is not to panic. Regulators have not concluded that these detected levels cause specific harm. But many parents are asking a simpler question: <strong>if the exposure can be reduced by changing the material that touches the milk, why not make that switch?</strong></p>
      <p>Here is the practical breakdown: what the research found, why &ldquo;BPA-free&rdquo; and some &ldquo;glass&rdquo; bottles may not fully solve the issue, and why Hosfu was built around the milk-contact surface &mdash; not just the outside of the bottle.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="source-box reveal">
      <b>Research note:</b> The core bottle-shedding data comes from Li et al., <em>Nature Food</em> 2020, which reported microplastic release from polypropylene infant feeding bottles under formula-preparation conditions. For ad-safety and accuracy, this page avoids claiming that microplastics cause a specific disease. References: <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s43016-020-00171-y" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nature Food study</a>, <a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/environmental-contaminants-food/microplastics-and-nanoplastics-foods" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDA position</a>, <a href="https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241516198" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WHO report</a>.
    </div>

    <section class="list">
      <article class="reason reveal">
        <div class="num">1</div>
        <h2>Your baby&rsquo;s bottle may be releasing microplastics into warm feeds</h2>
        <p>In 2020, researchers at Trinity College Dublin studied polypropylene infant feeding bottles &mdash; the plastic many baby bottles are made from. Following standard formula-preparation style conditions, they found releases reaching <strong>up to around 16 million microplastic particles per litre</strong>.</p>
        <p>That number sounds extreme, but the mechanism is simple: plastic, heat and repeated use do not stay perfectly unchanged forever. The more parents understand the material, the less &ldquo;BPA-free&rdquo; feels like the whole answer.</p>
      </article>
      <figure class="reason-media reveal">
        <img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0979/5082/6776/files/1_47b5be25-87fb-4713-994e-fd2ffca3d153.png?v=1783484878" alt="Microplastics concern in baby bottle feeding" width="1254" height="1254" loading="lazy">
      </figure>

      <article class="reason reveal">
        <div class="num">2</div>
        <h2>Hot water, sterilising and shaking are exactly the trigger points</h2>
        <p>The study found that temperature was one of the biggest drivers. When water got hotter, particle release increased sharply. That matters because feeding routines often include hot water, bottle warmers, sterilising, shaking and cleaning.</p>
        <p>In other words, the steps parents use to keep feeds clean can also be the steps that stress the plastic most. This is why the material touching the milk matters more than the marketing label on the front.</p>
      </article>
      <figure class="reason-media reveal">
        <img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0979/5082/6776/files/2_d71b52a4-80a9-4b31-b786-74d32d017945.png?v=1783484878" alt="Hot water and sterilising can stress plastic bottles" width="1254" height="1254" loading="lazy">
      </figure>

      <article class="reason reveal">
        <div class="num">3</div>
        <h2>&ldquo;BPA-free&rdquo; was never the same as &ldquo;plastic-free&rdquo;</h2>
        <p>BPA-free means one specific chemical &mdash; Bisphenol A &mdash; is not present. That is useful, but it does <strong>not</strong> mean the bottle is free from plastic, nor does it say anything about tiny particles physically shedding when plastic is heated, scrubbed or shaken.</p>
        <p>For years, &ldquo;BPA-free&rdquo; gave parents peace of mind. Now more parents are realising it answered only one question, not the whole bottle-safety question.</p>
      </article>
      <figure class="reason-media reveal">
        <img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0979/5082/6776/files/3_62c08158-4806-43ba-8c52-635d9c9f4e04.png?v=1783484878" alt="BPA-free does not mean plastic-free baby bottle" width="1254" height="1254" loading="lazy">
      </figure>

      <section class="cta-strip reveal">
        <h3>Want the simpler fix?</h3>
        <p>Hosfu is built around one practical rule: keep ordinary plastic away from the milk-contact path wherever possible.</p>
        <a class="cta" href="{{ product_link }}">See How Hosfu Fixes It</a>
        <p class="microcopy">Free shipping this week &middot; 90-day money-back guarantee</p>
      </section>

      <article class="reason reveal">
        <div class="num">4</div>
        <h2>It is not just one feed. It can repeat with daily washing and sterilising.</h2>
        <p>A bottle is not used once. It is washed, sterilised, warmed, cooled, packed, dropped, scrubbed and reused again. Tiny scratches and repeated heat exposure are exactly why parents are starting to think beyond &ldquo;is this bottle new?&rdquo; and ask &ldquo;what is this bottle made of?&rdquo;</p>
        <p>Plastic may look clean after every wash. But clean-looking and inert are not the same thing.</p>
      </article>
      <figure class="reason-media reveal">
        <img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0979/5082/6776/files/4_15b19733-c416-450a-9d2e-45308f304c8a.png?v=1783484878" alt="Daily washing and sterilising baby bottles" width="1254" height="1254" loading="lazy">
      </figure>

      <article class="reason reveal">
        <div class="num">5</div>
        <h2>Even some &ldquo;glass&rdquo; bottles still put plastic where it counts</h2>
        <p>Switching to glass feels like the obvious answer &mdash; until you look at the full milk path. Some bottles have a glass body, but still use plastic collars, vents, rings or bases that can sit near warm milk or steam during feeding and sterilising.</p>
        <p>That is why Hosfu focuses on the entire system, not just the bottle body. A safer-feeling material on the outside does not help much if the feed still passes through ordinary plastic parts.</p>
      </article>
      <figure class="reason-media reveal">
        <img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0979/5082/6776/files/5_ef7f9eee-cb3d-4694-9a5a-75b44d15c0a1.png?v=1783484878" alt="Glass bottle comparison with plastic parts" width="1254" height="1254" loading="lazy">
      </figure>

      <article class="reason reveal">
        <div class="num">6</div>
        <h2>Hosfu was designed around the milk-contact path</h2>
        <p>Most bottle pages talk about shape, size and cute colours. Hosfu starts with a more serious question: <strong>what touches the milk when the bottle is warm?</strong></p>
        <p>The Hosfu design is built around borosilicate glass and soft silicone for the surfaces that matter most, with heat-resistant components selected for daily sterilising routines. The goal is not to make feeding complicated &mdash; it is to remove the material guesswork from a routine parents repeat every day.</p>
